  "proposal": "filename: docs/rename-photos.md\n\n# Rename Photos with a Bash Loop\n\nRenaming a folder of holiday photos using a bash loop can be a quick and efficient way to organize your images. This guide will walk you through the process using different naming strategies.\n\n## Example One: Date Prefix\n\nThis example uses the date as a prefix for each photo. It ensures that the filenames are unique and include the date of the photo.\n\n```bash\nfor file in *.jpg; do\n  newname=$(date -r \"$file\" \"+%Y-%m-%d\")_$(basename \"$file\")\n  mv \"$file\" \"$newname\"\ndone\n```\n\n## Example Two: Lowercase Names\n\nThis example converts all filenames to lowercase, which is useful for consistency and compatibility with certain systems.\n\n```bash\nfor file in *.jpg; do\n  newname=$(echo \"$file\" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')\n  mv \"$file\" \"$newname\"\ndone\n```\n\n## Example Three: Number the Files\n\nThis example numbers the files sequentially, which is helpful for organizing photos in a specific order.\n\n```bash\ncounter=1\nfor file in *.jpg; do\n  newname=\"photo_$counter.jpg\"\n  mv \"$file\" \"$newname\"\n  counter=$((counter + 1))\ndone\n```\n\n## Wrap Up\n\nRenaming photos with bash loops can save time and help maintain an organized file structure. Each method serves a different purpose, so choose the one that best fits your needs. Always make sure to back up your files before making bulk changes. This guide provides a solid foundation for managing your holiday photos efficiently.",
